.TH SU 1.SH NAMEsu \- become another user.SH SYNOPSIS\fBsu\fR[\-flmp] [\-c command] [\-s shell] [\-] [user [argument ...]].SH DESCRIPTION\fBsu\fRis GNU su, from sh-utils 1.10.  It runs a shell as\fIuser\fR,\fRthe super user if none is named, after asking that user's password whenyou are not the super user yourself.  The user must be in\fB/dd/SYS/password\fR.\fRArguments after the user name are handed to that shell, so withMicroware's shell.nf    su tester whoami.firuns one command as \fBtester\fR and comes back..SH OPTIONS.TP 12\fB\-l, \-\fRa login shell..TP 12\fB\-c\fI command\fRpass \fB\-c\fI command\fR to the shell -- for a Unix-style shell; Microware'sshell does not take \fB\-c\fR, so give it the command as plain arguments..TP 12\fB\-m, \-p\fRkeep the environment as it is..TP 12\fB\-s\fI shell\fRrun\fIshell\fRinstead of the user's own..TP 12\fB\-f\fRpass\fB\-f\fRto the shell..SH NOTESNeeds Microware's\fBcio\fR.\fR\fBsu\fRstarts a module called\fBshell\fR,\fRthe one your own OS-9 brings.  Without one resident it answers 221,module not found, and becomes nobody..PPYour own OS-9 may have a utility of this name; see\fBDOC/README-NAMES\fR.\fR.SH SEE ALSOlogname, login